  "worked_solution": "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $2$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component ${t}^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t+1)$ is $t\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14093183/assets/44e047a975ee3b2ad7966b95bbba3a6270925f451c8cb8c6dac6dbef532f2b7a.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (-1,\\infty ).$",
